==Icelandic==
{{wikipedia|Ri-verbs}}
{{wikipedia|Ri-sögn|lang=is}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|snúa}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*snōwaną}}, from {{der|is|ine-pro|*sneh₁-|t=to wind; twist; braid; plait}}.

===Verb===
{{is-verb-ri|sneri|11=snéri|snúið}}

# {{lb|is|with dative}} to [[turn]]
# {{lb|is|with dative}} to [[face]]
#: {{ux|is|Hvor hliðin '''snýr''' niður?|Which side '''faces''' downwards?}}
# {{lb|is|with dative|informal}} to [[translate]], to [[render]]
# {{lb|is|with dative}} to [[change]] somebody's mind, to [[convert]] somebody
# {{lb|is|with dative}} to [[make]] someone run [[errands]]
# {{lb|is|with accusative}} to [[twine]], to [[twist]]

====Usage notes====
* One of the four [[w:is:Ri-sagnir|ri-verbs]] in Icelandic, the other being {{m|is|núa||to rub, massage; to wipe}}, {{m|is|gróa||to grow; to heal}} and {{m|is|róa||to row}}.
* As with the other ri-verbs, the past tense {{m|is|sneri||I turned}} was originally pronounced with an ''e'' sound but is today pronounced with a ''[[é]]'' sound. There are therefore two spelling variants used: {{m|is|sneri}} and {{m|is|snéri}}. The Icelandic Ministry of Education considers both variants to be equally correct,<ref>[https://ritreglur.arnastofnun.is/ Icelandic Ministry of Education - Announcements regarding orthography from 2016 and 2018, section 3.5.5]</ref> but many speakers consider the original {{m|is|sneri}} spelling to be the more correct one.

